  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWith a broad ridge out west and a trough in the east, the weather \nwill remain active during the midweek period. Beginning with today, \na MCS will moves through the Midwest and Great Lakes Region. Ahead \nof it, a weak wave will kick off some isolated showers and \nthunderstorms across northeast PA and possibly into portions of the \nSouthern Tier late this afternoon and evening. If skies stay mostly \ncloudy, then instability will likely be more limited than what most \nguidance have. However, if there can be some breaks in the clouds, \nthen MLCAPE values could rise to around 1000 J/kg. The 0-6 km bulk \nshear will be between 30 to 40 kts. As usual, while low-level lapse \nrates are steep, mid-level lapse rates will be modest at best. Given \nall of this, confidence is low for strong storms today though a \ncouple storms could become organized and have gusty winds as the \nmain hazard. \n\nOvernight, this system begins to drop south with the bulk of the \nprecipitation southwest of the region. However, many CAMs and some \nof larger-scale models have the system drift east some, leading to \nshowers across northeast PA for most, if not all, of the overnight \nperiod. The NAM does seem to be an outlier with the track of the \nsystem, though it should be noted that it hangs onto some \ninstability during the late evening hours. Typically conditions will \nbecome more stable after sunset as suggested by the GFS. Still, \nseeing the potential for some convection, isolated thunderstorms \ncannot be ruled out. PoPs were increased above NBM guidance for \ntonight. There is uncertainty how far north these showers will \nextend as a passing shower or two could be observed in the Southern \nTier. Patchy fog will be possible in the lower terrain areas as \nskies will be mostly clear and winds will be light. Locations that \nreceive rain today may have better chances due to the added moisture.\n\nHigh pressure will build back into the region on Wednesday, leading \nto a warm, dry day. The next weak disturbance will move through \novernight and support isolated to scattered showers. With limited \ninstability, a couple thunderstorms will also be possible. \nConditions will then dry out Thursday morning but with a deepening \nupper level trough, the next round of showers and thunderstorms \ndevelop during the afternoon. Showers will continue during the \novernight hours as a cold front drops south across the region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, the end of the week and most of the \nweekend will be dry and cooler. After a week of temperatures maxing \nout in the upper 70s to mid 80s, most of the region will see highs \nin the 70s heading into the weekend. While there remains uncertainty \nwith the timing of the next system, models have trended slightly \nslower. A weak low pressure system will move into the region by \nSunday night with the next round of showers and thunderstorms. Drier \nair is quick to follow this system, though wrap around moisture may \nbe enough to support some lingering showers on Monday.",
